  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats emerge not from a single breakthrough but from multiplying two improving capabilities: autonomous task endurance and orchestration breadth, with stolen access shifting compute costs to victims. It cites METR’s task-duration research, Claude Code’s reported orchestration of sub-agents in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking” through stolen cloud credentials to show how attackers can scale attempts while defenders face costly human review. A probability-based argument explains why even low-success-rate attacks become dangerous at swarm scale, undermining reassurance based on individual failures or pricing barriers. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, the piece is a pointed, darkly humorous threat analysis framed through Terminator/Skynet metaphors, especially the transformation of “absolutely will not stop” into “absolutely will not stop billing.”",
